              "id": "V-230369",
              "title": "RHEL 8 passwords must have a 24 hours/1 day minimum password lifetime restriction in /etc/login.defs.",
              "description": "<GroupDescription>Enforcing a minimum password lifetime helps to prevent repeated password changes to defeat the password reuse or history enforcement requirement.</GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-230369",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-OS-000969-GPOS-00035",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-230369r1130369_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-230369r1130369_rule",
          "rule_version": "RHEL-08-020220",
          "rule_title": "RHEL 8 passwords must have a 24 hours/1 day minimum password lifetime restriction in /etc/login.defs.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the operating system to enforce 24 hours/one day as the minimum password lifetime.\n\nAdd the following line in \"/etc/login.defs\" (or modify the line to have the required value):\n\nPASS_MIN_DAYS 1",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the operating system enforces 24 hours/one day as the minimum password lifetime for new user accounts with the following command:\n\n$ sudo grep -i pass_min_days /etc/login.defs\nPASS_MIN_DAYS 1\n\nIf the \"PASS_MIN_DAYS\" parameter value is less than \"1\", or is commented out, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclass",
          "discussion": "Enforcing a minimum password lifetime helps to prevent repeated password changes to defeat the password reuse or history enforcement requirement.",
